          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "4a5d5c2a-6e7e-3969-b284-7ea56f2cbe43",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. The use of a generic pointer type for the destination and the lack of explicit bounds or overlap checks further increase the risk. Additionally, the source may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. No explicit guard or evidence of safe intent is present. The source kind is not a string literal or C string, so the overlap risk is not reduced. This combination of factors makes the issue actionable and not a likely false positive.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(value, reinterpret_cast<const std::byte *>(&state_priv_reg) + offset, value_size);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination memory regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0a1e079f-6ca0-3b4b-a15e-49ed76774cf2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by data flow, which means that under certain conditions, they could refer to overlapping memory areas. This can cause unpredictable behavior, including data corruption or crashes. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this overlap, and the source may also be null, which further increases the risk. The classification specifically highlights undefined behavior due to possible overlap, and the verdict confirms this as a genuine issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with `memmove` using the same arguments:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(reinterpret_cast<std::byte *>(&status_reg) + offset, value, value_size);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination memory areas overlap, the operation will be performed safely without causing data corruption or crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c490d01e-9b4c-3725-8eba-3574f5dc6122",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by dataflow, which means there is a realistic chance that they may refer to overlapping memory at runtime. No explicit checks or guards are present to prevent this, and the source may also be null, increasing the risk of undefined behavior. The destination is not a pointer-based member, which slightly reduces the risk of null pointer dereference, but the primary concern remains the potential for memory overlap. This type of issue can lead to unpredictable program behavior, data corruption, or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with `memmove` using the same arguments:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(reinterpret_cast<std::byte *>(&status_reg) + offset, value, value_size);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination memory areas overlap, the operation will be performed safely without causing data corruption or crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(reinterpret_cast<std::byte *>(&status_reg) + offset, value, value_size);\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that `value` is not null and that `value_size` does not exceed the bounds of either the source or destination buffers. Add appropriate checks before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (value != nullptr && value_size > 0 /* && value_size within bounds */) {\n    memmove(reinterpret_cast<std::byte *>(&status_reg) + offset, value, value_size);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "31735895-2dbc-3305-95cb-cde688aeb30d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by data flow, which means they may refer to overlapping memory, and this can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this overlap, and the source may also be null, which further increases the risk. The source is a general identifier, not a string literal or constant, so the overlap risk is not mitigated by the nature of the source. All these factors indicate a real risk of undefined behavior and potential security or correctness issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the original call with `memmove` using the same arguments:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(reinterpret_cast<std::byte *>(&status_reg) + offset, value, value_size);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination memory areas overlap, the operation will be performed safely without causing data corruption or crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(reinterpret_cast<std::byte *>(&status_reg) + offset, value, value_size);\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that `value` is not null and that `value_size` does not exceed the bounds of either the source or destination buffers. Add appropriate checks before the call:\n\n```cpp\nif (value != nullptr && value_size > 0 /* && value_size within bounds */) {\n    memmove(reinterpret_cast<std::byte *>(&status_reg) + offset, value, value_size);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(reinterpret_cast<std::byte *>(&status_reg) + offset, value, value_size);\n```\n\nAdditionally, consider adding explicit checks to ensure that `value` is not null before performing the copy, and that the copy does not exceed the bounds of the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
