            {
              "id": "fdb673a9-5b34-3736-bd7d-19f5f2d2fde7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in a memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. The source and destination are not string literals or constant strings, which would reduce the risk of overlap. There is no explicit guard or check to prevent this overlap, and the analysis could not prove that the source or destination are always non-overlapping. Additionally, the source may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. These factors indicate a real risk of memory corruption or program instability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior caused by overlapping memory regions, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(ret, &value, sizeof(T));\n```\n\nAlternatively, review the logic to guarantee that `ret` and `&value` never overlap, and document this invariant clearly in the code. If you can guarantee non-overlap, add an assertion:\n\n```c\nassert((char*)ret + sizeof(T) <= (char*)&value || (char*)&value + sizeof(T) <= (char*)ret);\nmemcpy(ret, &value, sizeof(T));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2ae13ff7-6b1f-385d-b09c-20e32ac86256",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the `fatal_error` function is used, which is not a standard C or C++ library function, but it seems to be used for logging error messages. The function uses a format string which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if user-controlled input is passed to it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that are known to be insecure. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For format string vulnerabilities, do not pass user-controlled input to the format string. If you need to include user-controlled input in the error message, make sure to sanitize it first.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `fatal_error` is a function that takes a format string and arguments similar to `printf`, a safer version of the code would be:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cerrno>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <iostream>\n\nvoid fatal_error(const char* format, const char* error_message) {\n    // Implementation of fatal_error\n}\n\nint main() {\n    // Some code that might set errno\n    fatal_error(\"write: %s\", std::strerror(errno));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, `std::strerror` is used to convert the error number to a string, and this string is passed to `fatal_error`. This way, there is no user-controlled input in the format string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strerror`\n- `<iostream>`: for `std::cout`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fbf72609-d003-3979-9085-43ebe64405fd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions in the C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. The specific vulnerability sink in this case is the use of `strerror` function which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For instance, instead of `strerror`, use `std::strerror` which is thread-safe. Always validate and sanitize input data before processing. Implement proper error handling and logging mechanisms.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strerror` function with `std::strerror`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cerrno>\n\n// ...\n\nwarning (\"pipe_t::open: fcntl failed: %s\", std::strerror(errno));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strerror`\n- `<cstdio>`: for `warning`\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c1bf0f4f-9488-38a5-8d31-9fcb3ca20df4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the `fatal_error` function is used, which is not inherently insecure, but the way it is used can lead to format string vulnerabilities.\n\nFormat string vulnerabilities occur when user-supplied data is directly used as format specifiers in output functions. In this case, the `strerror` function is used to convert the `errno` value to a human-readable string, and this string is directly used as a format specifier in the `fatal_error` function. If an attacker can control the `errno` value, they can potentially exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using user-supplied data directly as format specifiers. Instead, use fixed strings as format specifiers and use user-supplied data only as arguments. This way, even if an attacker can control the user-supplied data, they cannot control the format specifiers and hence cannot exploit format string vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nfatal_error (\"read: %s\", strerror (errno));\n```\n\nShould be changed to:\n\n```cpp\nfatal_error (\"read error: %s\", strerror(errno));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following libraries:\n\n- `stdio.h` for the `strerror` function.\n- `errno.h` for the `errno` variable.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65448cd1-821b-3c9b-8808-c73fd4d60a04",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `strerror` function is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-reentrant functions like `strerror`. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives like `strerror_r` (POSIX) or `strerror_s` (Windows). Also, consider using higher-level abstractions provided by the C++ Standard Library, which are usually safer and less error-prone.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strerror` function with `strerror_r`:\n\n```cpp\nchar buf[1024];\nif (strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) != 0) {\n    // handle error\n}\nwarning(\"pipe_t::open: pipe2 failed: %s\", buf);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strerror` and `strerror_r`\n- `<cerrno>` for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "00855f7f-69b9-3621-8815-ac2d595a8293",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `vsprintf` function is used, which does not check the size of the output buffer and can lead to buffer overflow if the input is larger than the buffer size.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that include bounds checking to prevent buffer overflows. In this case, you can use `vsnprintf` instead of `vsprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `vsprintf` function with `vsnprintf` and provide the size of the buffer as an argument to prevent buffer overflow. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdarg>\n#include <cstdio>\n\nvoid safe_function(char *str, const char *format, ...) {\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(str, sizeof(str), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdarg>`: Provides macros to handle variable arguments.\n- `<cstdio>`: Provides functions for input and output.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "03aad710-b049-35e6-a317-286ff192b2c5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `vsnprintf` function is used, which is a variant of the `sprintf` function. It can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly.\n\nThe `vsnprintf` function writes output to the array according to a format string, but unlike `sprintf`, `vsnprintf` takes a maximum size argument to prevent buffer overflows. However, if the size argument is not correctly calculated or validated, it can still lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using C library functions that are prone to vulnerabilities. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you can use `std::snprintf` or `std::vsnprintf` from the `<cstdio>` library, which are safer versions of `sprintf` and `vsnprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdarg>\n\n// ...\n\nva_list args;\nva_start(args, format);\nsize = std::vsnprintf(NULL, 0, format, args);\nva_end(args);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: for `std::vsnprintf`\n- `<cstdarg>`: for `va_list`, `va_start`, and `va_end`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a5466758-1bd2-33f8-953b-e5ab88684aeb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code performs a memory copy operation without verifying that the destination pointer is not null. While the source pointer is guarded and cannot be null, the destination pointer may be null at this point in the code, as there is no evidence of a prior check. This can lead to undefined behavior, such as a crash or potential security issue, if the destination pointer is indeed null. The absence of a guard or check for the destination pointer's validity is the primary reason for this assessment.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the destination pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to handle the case where the destination pointer may be null, and take appropriate action (such as returning an error or allocating memory).\n\n```cpp\nif (retval.get() == nullptr) {\n    // Handle error: destination buffer is null\n    // For example, return early or throw an exception\n    return;\n}\nmemcpy (retval.get (), value.data (), size);\n```\n\nAlternatively, ensure that the logic leading to this call guarantees that the destination pointer is always valid and cannot be null.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e2fccfa2-0032-3fca-a566-b4c713169f5b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are derived from dataflow analysis, indicating that they may refer to overlapping memory. There is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this overlap. Additionally, the destination pointer may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. The source is confirmed to be non-null before the call, but this does not mitigate the overlap risk. The classification explicitly states a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap, and the verdict is marked as genuine.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, check that the destination pointer is not null before performing the copy operation.\n\n```cpp\nif (retval.get() != nullptr && value.data() != nullptr) {\n    // Use memmove to safely handle possible overlap\n    memmove(retval.get(), value.data(), size);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
          "contents": [
            "localizedData",
            "nonLocalizedData"
          ],
          "isComprehensive": false
        }
